<BaseLayout
  title="Orthopaedic Patient Guides | Dr. Aayush Shrestha"
  description="Read practical patient guides on back pain, neck pain, knee health, wound care, disc herniation and spine surgery from an orthopaedic surgeon in Nepal."
  canonical="https://draayushshrestha.com/patient-guides.html"
  active="patient-guides"
>
  <main id="main">
    <section class="shell page-hero guides-hero"><p class="kicker">Patient education</p><h1>Know your body. Ask better questions.</h1><p>Start with symptoms, understand the language used in clinic and know which changes should not wait.</p></section>
    <div class="body-guides">
      <section class="body-guide" id="spine-guide"><div class="shell"><div class="body-guide__lead"><div class="body-guide__copy"><p class="kicker">Back &amp; spine</p><h2>Spine</h2><p>Spinal conditions may be managed with non-surgical or surgical care depending on the diagnosis, symptoms and examination. The aim is to reduce pain, improve function and support a safe return to activity.</p></div><figure><img src="assets/images/neck-nerve-pain.jpeg" width="1440" height="1080" alt="Illustration of cervical nerve pain"></figure></div><details class="condition-disclosure"><summary>Show all spine conditions</summary><dl class="condition-definitions">
        <div><dt>Herniated disc</dt><dd>The soft centre of a spinal disc pushes through a crack in its tougher outer casing, causing pain, numbness or weakness in an arm or leg.</dd></div><div><dt>Degenerative disc disease</dt><dd>Wear and tear of spinal discs over time, leading to pain, stiffness and reduced flexibility.</dd></div><div><dt>Spinal stenosis</dt><dd>Narrowing of the spinal canal that can compress nerves and cause pain, numbness or weakness in the arms or legs.</dd></div><div><dt>Scoliosis</dt><dd>Abnormal spinal curvature that can produce uneven shoulders, hips or waist and may cause back pain or difficulty breathing.</dd></div><div><dt>Spondylolisthesis</dt><dd>A vertebra slips out of place, often because of a stress fracture, causing lower-back pain and sometimes leg pain or weakness.</dd></div><div><dt>Spinal fractures</dt><dd>Breaks or cracks in spinal bones resulting from trauma, osteoporosis or other conditions, with pain and possible nerve damage.</dd></div><div><dt>Sciatica</dt><dd>Compression or irritation of the sciatic nerve, causing pain that radiates from the lower back through the buttock and into the leg.</dd></div><div><dt>Spinal tumours</dt><dd>Abnormal growths in or near the spinal cord that can cause pain, weakness, numbness or bowel or bladder changes.</dd></div><div><dt>Facet joint syndrome</dt><dd>Degeneration or inflammation of the spine’s facet joints, leading to stiffness, pain and reduced range of motion.</dd></div><div><dt>Radiculopathy</dt><dd>Compression or irritation of a spinal nerve root, causing pain, numbness, tingling or weakness along the nerve’s pathway.</dd></div>
      </dl></details></div></section>
      <section class="body-guide body-guide--tinted" id="shoulder-guide"><div class="shell"><div class="body-guide__lead body-guide__lead--reverse"><div class="body-guide__copy"><p class="kicker">Upper limb</p><h2>Shoulder</h2><p>Shoulder pain needs professional assessment. Treatment may involve pain relief, physical therapy, rest or surgery, with the plan selected for the underlying cause and severity.</p></div><figure><img src="assets/images/shoulder-pain.png" width="359" height="381" loading="lazy" alt="People exercising their shoulders"></figure></div><details class="condition-disclosure"><summary>Show all shoulder conditions</summary><dl class="condition-definitions">
        <div><dt>Rotator cuff tears</dt><dd>Tears in rotator-cuff tendons caused by overuse, trauma or degeneration, resulting in pain, weakness and limited shoulder mobility.</dd></div><div><dt>Shoulder impingement syndrome</dt><dd>Compression of shoulder tendons and bursa, often from repetitive overhead movement or structural abnormalities, causing pain, inflammation and reduced motion.</dd></div><div><dt>Frozen shoulder</dt><dd>Thickening and tightening of the shoulder capsule and surrounding tissues, producing stiffness, pain and difficulty with daily activities.</dd></div><div><dt>Shoulder dislocation</dt><dd>Displacement of the upper-arm bone from the shoulder socket, usually from trauma or repetitive stress, causing severe pain and instability.</dd></div><div><dt>Shoulder instability</dt><dd>Excessive looseness in the shoulder joint that produces recurrent complete or partial dislocations, often because of ligament or labral tears.</dd></div><div><dt>Labral tears</dt><dd>Tears in the cartilage surrounding the shoulder socket, resulting in pain, instability and limited function.</dd></div><div><dt>Bursitis</dt><dd>Inflammation of fluid-filled bursae that cushion the joint, causing pain, swelling and limited mobility.</dd></div><div><dt>AC joint injuries</dt><dd>Sprains, separations or arthritis at the joint between the acromion and clavicle, causing pain, swelling and difficulty with overhead movement.</dd></div><div><dt>Shoulder arthritis</dt><dd>Degenerative or inflammatory joint changes resulting in pain, stiffness and reduced motion.</dd></div><div><dt>Biceps tendon disorders</dt><dd>Tendinitis, tendinosis or tears involving the long head of the biceps tendon, causing pain, weakness and limited overhead function.</dd></div>
      </dl></details></div></section>
      <section class="body-guide" id="knee-guide"><div class="shell"><div class="body-guide__lead"><div class="body-guide__copy"><p class="kicker">Joint health</p><h2>Knee</h2><p>Knee pain may be treated with physical therapy, injections, bracing or surgery. Care should be matched to the condition, activity needs and examination findings.</p></div><figure><img src="assets/images/knee-osteoarthritis.png" width="619" height="412" loading="lazy" alt="Illustration of osteoarthritis in the knee"></figure></div><details class="condition-disclosure"><summary>Show all knee conditions</summary><dl class="condition-definitions">
        <div><dt>ACL injury</dt><dd>ACL tears or sprains, often from sport or sudden twisting, causing instability, swelling and pain and sometimes requiring reconstruction.</dd></div><div><dt>Meniscus tears</dt><dd>Tears in the cartilage pads cushioning the knee, generally caused by twisting or direct trauma, producing pain, swelling, stiffness and possible locking.</dd></div><div><dt>Patellar dislocation or subluxation</dt><dd>Complete or partial displacement of the kneecap from the femoral groove, causing pain, swelling and instability.</dd></div><div><dt>Patellar tendinitis</dt><dd>Irritation of the tendon connecting the kneecap to the tibia, often from repetitive jumping or overuse, causing front-of-knee pain and stiffness.</dd></div><div><dt>Patellofemoral pain syndrome</dt><dd>Pain around or behind the kneecap during knee-bending activities, associated with abnormal kneecap tracking or alignment.</dd></div><div><dt>Osteoarthritis</dt><dd>Degeneration of knee cartilage through wear and tear, causing pain, stiffness, swelling and reduced mobility.</dd></div><div><dt>Rheumatoid arthritis</dt><dd>Autoimmune inflammatory arthritis of the knee, producing pain, swelling, stiffness and possible joint deformity.</dd></div><div><dt>Iliotibial band syndrome</dt><dd>Irritation of the tissue band along the outside of the thigh, usually from repetitive motion, causing outer-knee pain during running or cycling.</dd></div><div><dt>PCL injury</dt><dd>PCL tears or sprains from front-of-knee impact or hyperextension, causing posterior pain, swelling and instability.</dd></div><div><dt>Knee fractures</dt><dd>Fractures of the femur, tibia or patella around the knee, causing pain, swelling, bruising and difficulty bearing weight.</dd></div>
      </dl></details></div></section>
      <section class="body-guide body-guide--tinted" id="foot-ankle-guide"><div class="shell"><div class="body-guide__lead body-guide__lead--reverse"><div class="body-guide__copy"><p class="kicker">Lower limb</p><h2>Foot &amp; ankle</h2><p>Foot and ankle pain can stem from injury, a medical condition or overuse. Treatment may include rest, physical therapy, medication or surgery depending on the cause and severity.</p></div><figure><img src="assets/images/foot-ankle.jpg" width="617" height="349" loading="lazy" alt="Person holding a painful foot after exercise"></figure></div><details class="condition-disclosure"><summary>Show all foot and ankle conditions</summary><dl class="condition-definitions">
        <div><dt>Ankle sprains</dt><dd>Stretching or tearing of ankle-supporting ligaments, often from a sudden twist, causing pain, swelling and instability.</dd></div><div><dt>Plantar fasciitis</dt><dd>Inflammation of the tissue band along the bottom of the foot, typically causing heel pain with first morning steps or after rest.</dd></div><div><dt>Achilles tendinitis</dt><dd>Inflammation of the Achilles tendon connecting the calf muscles to the heel, causing pain and stiffness along the back of the leg and heel.</dd></div><div><dt>Bunions</dt><dd>Bony bumps at the base of the big toe associated with footwear, genetics or structural deformity, causing pain and difficulty wearing shoes.</dd></div><div><dt>Ingrown toenails</dt><dd>A toenail edge grows into surrounding skin, causing pain, redness, swelling and sometimes infection.</dd></div><div><dt>Metatarsalgia</dt><dd>Pain and inflammation in the ball of the foot caused by overuse, footwear or structural abnormalities, sometimes with numbness or burning.</dd></div><div><dt>Hammer toe</dt><dd>Abnormal bending of a toe, causing pain, corns, calluses and difficulty fitting shoes.</dd></div><div><dt>Flat feet</dt><dd>Collapse of the foot’s arch, producing inward rolling and potentially causing pain, fatigue or impaired balance.</dd></div><div><dt>High arches</dt><dd>An abnormally high arch that places excess pressure on the heel and forefoot, increasing pain and instability.</dd></div><div><dt>Tarsal tunnel syndrome</dt><dd>Compression of the tibial nerve inside the ankle, causing pain, numbness, tingling or weakness in the foot and toes.</dd></div>
      </dl></details></div></section>
      <section class="body-guide" id="elbow-guide"><div class="shell"><div class="body-guide__lead"><div class="body-guide__copy"><p class="kicker">Upper limb</p><h2>Elbow</h2><p>Elbow pain can result from overuse, trauma, arthritis or tendinitis. Treatment may include rest, physical therapy, medication or surgery.</p></div><figure><img src="assets/images/elbow.jpg" width="1183" height="887" loading="lazy" alt="Person supporting a painful elbow"></figure></div><details class="condition-disclosure"><summary>Show all elbow conditions</summary><dl class="condition-definitions">
        <div><dt>Tennis elbow</dt><dd>Inflammation or degeneration of tendons outside the elbow, usually from repetitive wrist and forearm movement, causing pain and tenderness.</dd></div><div><dt>Golfer’s elbow</dt><dd>Inflammation or degeneration of tendons inside the elbow, usually from repetitive gripping and swinging.</dd></div><div><dt>Elbow bursitis</dt><dd>Inflammation of fluid-filled bursae around the elbow, often from repetitive pressure or trauma, causing pain and swelling.</dd></div><div><dt>Olecranon fracture</dt><dd>Fracture of the bony prominence at the back of the elbow, usually from a direct blow or fall.</dd></div><div><dt>Elbow dislocation</dt><dd>Displacement of bones forming the elbow joint, typically after trauma or a fall, causing severe pain and deformity.</dd></div><div><dt>Radial head fracture</dt><dd>Fracture of the upper radius near the elbow, often after a fall on an outstretched hand.</dd></div><div><dt>Olecranon bursitis</dt><dd>Inflammation of the bursa over the olecranon, causing pain, swelling and a visible lump at the back of the elbow.</dd></div><div><dt>Elbow arthritis</dt><dd>Degenerative or inflammatory joint changes causing pain, stiffness, swelling and reduced motion.</dd></div><div><dt>Ulnar nerve entrapment</dt><dd>Compression of the ulnar nerve in the cubital tunnel, causing pain, numbness, tingling or weakness in the forearm and hand.</dd></div><div><dt>Elbow tendon ruptures</dt><dd>Tears of biceps, triceps or other tendons around the elbow, causing pain, weakness and impaired function.</dd></div>
      </dl></details></div></section>
      <section class="body-guide body-guide--tinted" id="hip-guide"><div class="shell"><div class="body-guide__lead body-guide__lead--reverse"><div class="body-guide__copy"><p class="kicker">Joint health</p><h2>Hip</h2><p>Hip pain has many causes, including arthritis, bursitis, tendinitis, fractures, impingement and labral injury. Assessment helps identify the underlying cause.</p></div><figure><img src="assets/images/hip-pain.jpg" width="268" height="188" loading="lazy" alt="Person holding a painful hip"></figure></div><details class="condition-disclosure"><summary>Show all hip conditions</summary><dl class="condition-definitions">
        <div><dt>Hip osteoarthritis</dt><dd>Breakdown of cartilage in the hip joint, causing pain, stiffness, swelling and reduced motion during weight-bearing activity.</dd></div><div><dt>Hip fractures</dt><dd>Breaks in hip bones, often from falls, trauma or osteoporosis, causing severe pain and inability to bear weight.</dd></div><div><dt>Hip bursitis</dt><dd>Inflammation of the sacs cushioning the hip, usually from overuse, trauma or prolonged pressure, causing outer-hip pain.</dd></div><div><dt>Hip labral tears</dt><dd>Tears in the cartilage ring lining the socket, causing pain, clicking or catching and reduced mobility.</dd></div><div><dt>Femoroacetabular impingement</dt><dd>Abnormal contact between the femoral head and socket, causing pain, stiffness and reduced movement.</dd></div><div><dt>Hip dysplasia</dt><dd>Abnormal hip-joint development that causes instability and increases the risk of dislocation or osteoarthritis.</dd></div><div><dt>Avascular necrosis</dt><dd>Death of hip bone tissue from poor blood supply, eventually causing pain, stiffness and joint-surface collapse.</dd></div><div><dt>Hip tendinitis</dt><dd>Irritation of tendons around the hip from overuse or a sudden activity increase, causing pain and weakness.</dd></div><div><dt>Hip dislocation</dt><dd>Displacement of the femoral head from the socket after trauma, causing severe pain and inability to move the joint.</dd></div><div><dt>Snapping hip syndrome</dt><dd>Audible or palpable snapping during hip movement, usually from muscles or tendons rubbing over bone.</dd></div>
      </dl></details></div></section>
      <section class="body-guide" id="hand-wrist-guide"><div class="shell"><div class="body-guide__lead"><div class="body-guide__copy"><p class="kicker">Upper limb</p><h2>Hand &amp; wrist</h2><p>Repetitive use, arthritis, injury, tendinitis, posture and health conditions such as diabetes can contribute to hand and wrist symptoms.</p></div><figure><img src="assets/images/hand-wrist.png" width="744" height="516" loading="lazy" alt="Illustration of weakness and pain in the hand"></figure></div><details class="condition-disclosure"><summary>Show all hand and wrist conditions</summary><dl class="condition-definitions">
        <div><dt>Carpal tunnel syndrome</dt><dd>Compression of the median nerve at the wrist, causing pain, numbness, tingling and weakness in the thumb and adjacent fingers.</dd></div><div><dt>Trigger finger</dt><dd>Inflammation or thickening of a finger’s tendon sheath, causing the finger to catch or lock in a bent position.</dd></div><div><dt>De Quervain’s tenosynovitis</dt><dd>Inflammation of tendons at the base of the thumb, causing pain, swelling and difficulty gripping.</dd></div><div><dt>Ganglion cysts</dt><dd>Noncancerous fluid-filled lumps along tendons or joints that can cause pain and restricted movement.</dd></div><div><dt>Dupuytren’s contracture</dt><dd>Progressive thickening beneath the palm that forms cords and pulls fingers into a bent position.</dd></div><div><dt>Wrist sprains</dt><dd>Stretching or tearing of wrist ligaments, often from falls or sudden twisting.</dd></div><div><dt>Fractures</dt><dd>Breaks in hand or wrist bones from trauma, falls or repetitive stress, causing pain, swelling and sometimes deformity.</dd></div><div><dt>Arthritis</dt><dd>Degenerative or inflammatory joint disease causing pain, stiffness, swelling and reduced motion.</dd></div><div><dt>Tendinitis or tendinosis</dt><dd>Inflammation or degeneration of hand or wrist tendons caused by overuse, repetitive movement or injury.</dd></div><div><dt>Cubital tunnel syndrome</dt><dd>Compression of the ulnar nerve at the elbow, causing symptoms in the hand, particularly the ring and little fingers.</dd></div>
      </dl></details></div></section>
      <section class="body-guide body-guide--tinted" id="rheumatic-guide"><div class="shell"><div class="body-guide__lead body-guide__lead--reverse"><div class="body-guide__copy"><p class="kicker">Systemic conditions</p><h2>Rheumatic conditions</h2><p>Rheumatic diseases can cause joint and muscle pain, swelling, stiffness and systemic symptoms. Treatment varies and may involve medication, therapy or surgery.</p></div><figure><img src="assets/images/rheumatic-conditions.jpg" width="1254" height="836" loading="lazy" alt="Person experiencing inflammatory joint pain"></figure></div><details class="condition-disclosure"><summary>Show all rheumatic conditions</summary><dl class="condition-definitions">
        <div><dt>Rheumatoid arthritis</dt><dd>Chronic autoimmune disease causing joint inflammation, pain, swelling and stiffness.</dd></div><div><dt>Ankylosing spondylitis</dt><dd>Inflammatory arthritis primarily affecting the spine, causing stiffness, pain and reduced mobility.</dd></div><div><dt>Psoriatic arthritis</dt><dd>Arthritis occurring in some people with psoriasis, producing joint inflammation plus skin and nail changes.</dd></div><div><dt>Systemic lupus erythematosus</dt><dd>Autoimmune disease affecting multiple organs and joints, often with rashes, fatigue and systemic symptoms.</dd></div><div><dt>Gout</dt><dd>Arthritis caused by uric-acid crystals, producing sudden severe joint pain, redness, swelling and warmth.</dd></div><div><dt>Juvenile idiopathic arthritis</dt><dd>A group of inflammatory joint disorders arising before age 16, causing pain, stiffness and reduced mobility.</dd></div><div><dt>Fibromyalgia</dt><dd>Chronic widespread musculoskeletal pain with fatigue, sleep disturbance and tender areas.</dd></div><div><dt>Sjögren’s syndrome</dt><dd>Autoimmune disease causing dry eyes and mouth and potentially joint pain, inflammation and fatigue.</dd></div><div><dt>Polymyalgia rheumatica</dt><dd>Inflammatory condition causing shoulder, neck, hip and thigh pain and stiffness, generally in older adults.</dd></div><div><dt>Temporal arteritis</dt><dd>Arterial inflammation causing headache, scalp tenderness, jaw pain and vision changes, requiring prompt medical care.</dd></div>
      </dl></details></div></section>
      <section class="body-guide" id="trauma-guide"><div class="shell"><div class="body-guide__lead"><div class="body-guide__copy"><p class="kicker">Urgent care</p><h2>Trauma</h2><p>Orthopaedic trauma care provides prompt evaluation and treatment after physical injury, aiming to prevent long-term complications and restore function.</p></div><figure><img src="assets/images/trauma.png" width="512" height="512" loading="lazy" alt="Illustration representing orthopaedic trauma care"></figure></div><details class="condition-disclosure"><summary>Show all trauma conditions</summary><dl class="condition-definitions">
        <div><dt>Fractures</dt><dd>Breaks or cracks in bones caused by falls, sports injuries, accidents or other trauma.</dd></div><div><dt>Dislocations</dt><dd>Joint injuries in which bones are forced out of their normal positions, causing severe pain and loss of function.</dd></div><div><dt>Sprains and strains</dt><dd>Overstretching or tearing of ligaments, muscles or tendons during sport or sudden movement.</dd></div><div><dt>Soft-tissue injuries</dt><dd>Damage around bones, including bruises, contusions, lacerations and abrasions.</dd></div><div><dt>Traumatic amputations</dt><dd>Severe injuries causing partial or complete limb loss and requiring immediate specialist care.</dd></div><div><dt>Crush injuries</dt><dd>Damage from substantial force or pressure, potentially causing tissue injury, compartment syndrome and loss of function.</dd></div><div><dt>Open wounds</dt><dd>Injuries that break the skin and expose deeper tissues, increasing infection risk and requiring prompt care.</dd></div><div><dt>Polytrauma</dt><dd>Multiple severe injuries across different body regions, often requiring multidisciplinary management and rehabilitation.</dd></div><div><dt>Traumatic joint injuries</dt><dd>Ligament, meniscus, cartilage or other joint injuries that may require surgical stabilisation or repair.</dd></div><div><dt>Complications of trauma</dt><dd>Nerve damage, vascular injury, compartment syndrome or post-traumatic arthritis requiring specialised treatment.</dd></div>
      </dl></details></div></section>
    </div>
    <section class="section"><div class="narrow"><div class="medical-note"><strong>Medical information notice</strong><p>These guides are educational and do not provide a diagnosis or individual treatment plan. Advice may differ after surgery or for people with other health conditions. Seek urgent care for severe or rapidly worsening symptoms.</p></div></div></section>
    <section class="section section--dark"><div class="shell cta-band"><h2>A guide can inform. An examination can diagnose.</h2><div><p>Bring prior reports and imaging to your appointment.</p><p class="action-block"><a class="btn btn--light" href="contact.html">Book appointment</a></p></div></div></section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
